A presidential election was held in Portugal on 22 January 2006 to elect a successor to the incumbent [[President of Portugal|President]] [[Jorge Sampaio]], who was [[term limit|barred]] from running for a third consecutive term by the [[Constitution of Portugal]]. The result was a narrow first-round victory for former [[Prime Minister of Portugal|prime minister]] and candidate supported by the [[Social Democratic Party (Portugal)|Social Democratic Party]] (PSD), [[Aníbal Cavaco Silva]], who obtained 50.54 percent of the votes, just over the [[majority]] required to avoid a [[Two-round system|runoff election]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2006-01-31 |title=Resultados oficiais dão vitória a Cavaco Silva com 50,54 por cento |url=https://www.publico.pt/2006/01/31/politica/noticia/resultados-oficiais-dao-vitoria-a-cavaco-silva-com-5054-por-cento-1246441 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref> 

It was the first time in which a right-wing candidate was elected President of the Republic since the 1974 [[Carnation Revolution]], and this election would also mark the beginning of a 20-year period in which the PSD held the presidency. Former president, prime minister and PS leader, [[Mário Soares]], suffered a massive defeat, polling third with just 14 percent of the votes, while being surpassed by his party colleague, [[Manuel Alegre]], who came in second with almost 21 percent of the votes.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2006-01-23 |title=Mário Soares assumiu derrota com "fair play", mas omitiu Manuel Alegre |url=https://www.publico.pt/2006/01/23/politica/noticia/mario-soares-assumiu-derrota-com-fair-play-mas-omitiu-manuel-alegre-1245592 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2006-01-23 |title=Manuel Alegre: um milhão de votos "contra ventos e marés, contra tudo e contra todos"  |url=https://www.publico.pt/2006/01/23/politica/noticia/manuel-alegre-um-milhao-de-votos-contra-ventos-e-mares-contra-tudo-e-contra-todos-1245596 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref>

[[Voter turnout]] was 62 percent. Cavaco Silva was sworn in as President on 9 March 2006.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/posse-de-cavaco-silva-como-presidente-da-republica/|title=Posse de Cavaco Silva como Presidente da República |language=pt |work=RTP Arquivos |access-date=18 February 2026 |date=9 March 2006}}</ref>

==Background==
In the presidential election of 14 January 2001, the outgoing Socialist [[Jorge Sampaio]] was re-elected in the first round with 55 percent of votes.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2001-01-14 |title=Sampaio reeleito com 55,8 por cento dos votos |url=https://www.publico.pt/2001/01/14/politica/noticia/sampaio-reeleito-com-558-por-cento-dos-votos-7072 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref> Because he was term-limited, he was forbidden by the Constitution to run for a third consecutive term.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2005-12-15 |title="Abençoada Constituição" por proibir terceiro mandato |url=https://www.jn.pt/arquivo/artigo/abencoada-constituicao-por-proibir-terceiro-mandato/527155 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Jornal de Notícias |language=pt}}</ref> Sampaio's second term began with the continuation of the institutional relationship with Socialist prime minister António Guterres, which ended after the [[2002 Portuguese legislative election|early election of March 2002]], when a new [[Cohabitation (government)|cohabitation]] started, now with a PSD Prime Minister, [[José Manuel Durão Barroso]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2003-11-12 |title=Um teste à coabitação  |url=https://www.publico.pt/2003/11/12/jornal/um-teste-a-coabitacao-207547 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref> Portugal's role in the [[Iraq War]], in which Barroso's government sided with the [[United States of America|USA]], created some discomfort between the government and the president.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-09-10 |title=Os temas que marcaram a passagem de Jorge Sampaio por Belém  |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/politica/os-temas-que-marcaram-a-passagem-de-jorge-sampaio-por-belem_es1060892 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref> In the summer of 2004, Barroso resigned in order to become [[President of the European Commission]],<ref>[https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/politica/durao-barroso-apresenta-demissao-para-ser-presidente-da-comissao-europeia_v853581 "Durão Barroso apresenta demissão para ser presidente da Comissão Europeia "], ''RTP'', 8 September 2015. Retrieved 26 August 2026.</ref> and Sampaio nominated the Social Democrats' new leader, [[Pedro Santana Lopes]], as prime minister,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2004-07-15 |title=Governo de Santana Lopes toma posse na próxima terça-feira |url=https://tvi.iol.pt/noticias/jorge-sampaio/primeiro-ministro/governo-de-santana-lopes-toma-posse-na-proxima-terca-feira |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=TVI |language=pt}}</ref> against the objection of the PS.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2016-01-15 |title=O dia em que Jorge Sampaio decepcionou os socialistas |url=https://expresso.pt/presidenciais2016/2016-01-15-O-dia-em-que-Jorge-Sampaio-decepcionou-os-socialistas |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Expresso |language=pt}}</ref> A few months later, a series of controversies within Santana's government, led to a split between president and prime minister, with Sampaio dismissing the government and calling a snap election.<ref>[https://www.cmjornal.pt/politica/detalhe/a-bomba-atomica-de-sampaio "A bomba atómica de Sampaio"], ''Correio da Manhã'', 10 September 2021. Retrieved 26 August 2026.</ref> In the [[2005 Portuguese legislative election|parliamentary election of 20 February 2005]], the Socialist Party (PS), led by [[José Sócrates]], won for the first time in its history an absolute majority of seats,<ref name="PSPSD05">{{Cite web |date=2005-02-21 |title=PS conquista primeira maioria absoluta da sua história |url=https://www.publico.pt/2005/02/21/politica/noticia/ps-conquista-primeira-maioria-absoluta-da-sua-historia-1216407 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref> while the [[Social Democratic Party (Portugal)|Social Democratic Party]] (PSD), led by Pedro Santana Lopes, fell below 30 percent, one of their worst result since 1983.<ref name="PSPSD05" /> The last year of Sampaio's second term was, once again, of coexistence with a prime minister from his own party.

===Pre-campaign===

In the follow-up for the presidential election, the candidacy of former PSD prime minister [[Aníbal Cavaco Silva]] (1985–1995) was seen as likely for months, until its confirmation in October 2005.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2005-10-20 |title=Presidenciais: Cavaco Silva anuncia candidatura |url=https://www.jpn.up.pt/2005/10/20/presidenciais-cavaco-silva-anuncia-candidatura/ |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Jornalismo Porto Net |language=pt}}</ref> On the Socialist side, the vacuum left by Sampaio's departure and Cavaco's candidacy split the party, resulting in two candidacies from the same political camp, former secretary-general and President of the Republic between 1986 and 1996, [[Mário Soares]], and party member [[Manuel Alegre]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2005-08-31 |title=Mário Soares apresenta hoje candidatura à Presidência da República |url=https://www.publico.pt/2005/08/31/politica/noticia/mario-soares-apresenta-hoje-candidatura-a-presidencia-da-republica-1231685 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref><ref name="MA2006">{{Cite web |date=2005-10-24 |title=Manuel Alegre é candidato às presidenciais de 2006 |url=https://www.publico.pt/2005/09/24/politica/noticia/manuel-alegre-e-candidato-as-presidenciais-de-2006-1233734 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref> The then leaders of the [[Portuguese Communist Party|Communist Party]] and the [[Left Bloc]], [[Jerónimo de Sousa]] and [[Francisco Louçã]], respectively, also announced their candidacies.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2021-01-20 |title=Memória das Presidenciais - 2006  |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/politica/memoria-das-presidenciais-2006_a1291046 |access-date=2026-08-25 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
Any [[Portuguese nationality law|Portuguese citizen]] over 35 years old has the opportunity to run for president. In order to do so it is necessary to gather between 7,500 and 15,000 signatures and submit them to the [[Portuguese Constitutional Court]].

According to the [[Constitution of Portugal|Portuguese Constitution]], to be elected, a candidate needs a majority of votes. If no candidate gets this majority there will take place a second round between the two most voted candidates.

== Candidates ==
Thirteen citizens sought election officially, but only six gathered the 7,500 signatures required under the constitution to be a candidate in the poll. All the candidates except for Cavaco Silva are considered to be from the Portuguese political [[Left (politics)|left]].

